# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Naming conventions
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   packages  named for the QUESTION they answer, not the layer they sit in:
#             scheduling/ = "when does it come back", session/ = "what now"
#   modules   snake_case, named for the noun they own (queue_builder.py)
#   classes   PascalCase, named for the role played (Sm2Policy, JsonCardRepository)
#   tests     test_<module under test>.py, mirroring the source layout exactly
#   fixtures  named for the CONDITION they represent (malformed_deck.json), so a
#             failing test names its own scenario
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Import path strategy
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   - Run as `python main.py review spanish_basics --policy sm2` from the project
#     root; tests run with `pytest` from the same root, so both resolve the
#     package imports identically with no sys.path manipulation.
#   - domain/* imports NOTHING from the package. It is the graph's only leaf, which
#     is what guarantees the design is acyclic.
#   - scheduling/*, storage/*, and session/* import from `interfaces` and `domain`
#     only. They never import each other's concrete classes.
#   - review_session imports the ABCs from `interfaces`, never sm2/leitner or
#     json_repository; main.py supplies the concrete instances. This is what keeps
#     the session unit-testable with fakes and no filesystem.
#   - NOTHING imports datetime.now() outside utils/clock.py. Every other module
#     takes a Clock. Enforced by a grep in CI, because a single violation makes a
#     scheduling test flaky in a way that is very hard to diagnose later.
#   - Nothing imports main.py, which keeps the entry point free to change.
